            <ArticleTitle>[Report of a Case of Long-Term Survival with Mulitidisciplinary Therapy for a Patient with Multiple Liver Metastases from Rectal Cancer].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2154-2156</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Multidisciplinary therapy is necessary to prevent recurrence of advanced rectal cancer and advanced cancer with metastases. Here we report a case of long-term survival of a patient with advanced rectal cancer with multiple liver metastases. An 80's woman had previously undergone both Hartmann's operation and a partial hepatectomy for advanced rectal cancer with multiple liver metastases. A year after chemotherapy, a CT scan revealed multiple liver metastases. Thus, we performed partial liver resection. After another round of chemotherapy, a CT scan revealed lung metastases and local recurrence of the rectal cancer; therefore, we performed partial lung resection and a Miles operation. These procedures were conducted 4 years after her first operation. The following year, PET-CT revealed a mediastinum lymph node metastasis; consequently, we performed radiation therapy. New lung metastases and local recurrences of rectal cancer were identified after the radiation therapy; thus, we resumed the therapy, including a molecular targeting drug. Although the patient is in a tumor-bearing state, she is still alive 10 years after her first operation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
